      "text": "To solve this problem, we can use the concept of graph theory. We can represent the men and women as vertices of a graph, and the dances as edges between the vertices. Since each man danced with exactly three women, we can say that each vertex has a degree of 3. Similarly, since each woman danced with exactly two men, we can say that each vertex has a degree of 2.\n\nThe sum of the degrees of all the vertices in a graph is equal to twice the number of edges. In this case, we have 12 men and 12 women, so the sum of the degrees of all the vertices is 24. Since each edge represents a dance between a man and a woman, we can say that the number of edges (dances) is 12.\n\nSince each woman danced with exactly two men, we can say that the number of edges (dances) is equal to the number of women multiplied by the degree of each woman. Therefore, we can write the equation:\n\n12 = 12 * 2\n\nSolving for the number of women, we find that there are 12 women at the party.",
